Take the console's keyboard and display modes while the server runs.

The "stub" os-support backend has xf86OpenConsole()/xf86CloseConsole() as
no-ops, because there are no virtual terminals.  But the console has the KD
modes, and they are how a display server tells the kernel that the screen and
the keyboard are its own now:

  - KD_GRAPHICS: the console's pointer (scrollbar, wheel scrollback, terminal
    mouse reports) stays out of the way;
  - K_OFF: keys are no longer also typed into the console -- into the shell
    that started the server, unseen, to run when the session ends.  The event
    devices the input drivers read still get every key.

So xf86OpenConsole() now opens the console (/dev/tty0, else /dev/console),
saves KDGETMODE and KDGKBMODE, and sets KD_GRAPHICS and K_OFF (K_RAW with the
console drained if K_OFF is refused), as the VT console layer upstream ships
does -- without its VT switch and terminal attributes.  xf86CloseConsole(),
called from ddxGiveUp() on a normal exit and on AbortServer() (FatalError,
fatal signals), puts both modes back and closes the descriptor.

The descriptor is opened while the server is still root (InitOutput, before
patch 0005 drops privileges) and stays open; the kernel lets the file that
changed the modes change them back after the drop.  A server that dies
without reaching xf86CloseConsole() (SIGKILL) is covered by the kernel: it
restores both modes when the file that set them is finally closed.  O_CLOEXEC
so that a child (xkbcomp, the session) never holds the console in its place.

